   "answer": "Based on the current lecture, here is how SRAM and DRAM differ in data storage and their typical uses:\n\n## How They Store Data\n\n**SRAM (Static RAM)** [S3, p.32]:\n- Stores data via a **stable transistor circuit** (a bistable latch).\n- Requires **6 transistors per bit**, making it \"expansive\" (larger area per bit).\n- Needs a small amount of power to retain the bit (e.g., 423 mV for a 45 nm process).\n- Reading is **non-destructive**: the cell pulls one of the bit lines, and a sense amplifier on the column detects the difference between `bit` and `bitbar` [S1, p.31].\n\n**DRAM (Dynamic RAM)** [S3, p.32]:\n- Stores data as **tiny capacitor charges** — a bit is represented as charged (1) or discharged (0).\n- Uses only **one transistor per bit**, making it cheap and dense.\n- The stored charge is weak, so the cell must be **re-written after being read** (destructive read) and **periodically refreshed** [S3, p.32].\n- Address lines are multiplexed: the upper half of the address is the **Row Address Strobe (RAS)** and the lower half is the **Column Address Strobe (CAS)** [S3, p.32].\n\n## Why DRAM Is Slower (supporting context) [S2, p.35]\n- **Weak storage signal:** capacitor charges produce only a small voltage difference on long bitlines.\n- **Sensing takes time:** the sense amplifier must amplify a tiny voltage change into a full 0/1.\n- **Destructive read:** data must be restored after every row activation.\n- **Large dense arrays:** long rows/bitlines introduce higher RC delay.\n\n## Typical Uses [S3, p.32]\n- **SRAM:** Caches and register files (fast, on-chip memory).\n- **DRAM:** Main memory technologies such as **DDR / GDDR / HBM**.\n\n## Refresh Detail (DRAM) [S4, p.34]\n- DRAM storage is \"leaky,\" so it requires periodic refresh.\n- Example given: 4K rows with a 100 ns read cycle → 4096 × 100 ns ≈ 410 µs to refresh once; over a 64 ms window this is about 0.64% unavailability.\n- The lecture notes two refresh strategies: **Bursty Refresh** and **Distributed Refresh**.\n\n**Summary:** SRAM uses a 6-transistor stable circuit (fast, non-destructive reads, used for caches/register files), while DRAM uses a 1-transistor + capacitor cell (cheap, dense, but requires destructive reads and periodic refresh, used for main memory like DDR/GDDR/HBM).\n\nSources: [S1] p.31, [S2] p.35, [S3] p.32, [S4] p.34.",
